Subject: [PATCH] wineandroid.drv: Add a Vulkan driver.

Vulkan surfaces are Android surfaces made on the window's client
ANativeWindow, the same proxy window the OpenGL path renders into.
Wine's generated Vulkan tables don't know VK_KHR_android_surface, so
win32u enables it by name on Android in place of VK_KHR_win32_surface.
Java creates the client surface asynchronously, so surface creation
waits for it; until then libvulkan's connect fails with
VK_ERROR_NATIVE_WINDOW_IN_USE_KHR.

Android's libvulkan swapchain needs more of the ANativeWindow interface
than EGL does: 64-bit usage, dataspace, shared buffer mode, auto refresh,
dequeue timeout, refresh duration, consumer usage and frame ids, plus
the usage64 and layerCount fields of window buffers. Android 17 also
connects with op 57 (connect with listener, no listener given), sets a
present mode (53) and on-acquired/dropped callbacks (55/56); those
aren't in AOSP yet and were read from the device's libvulkan. The proxy
forwards the setters and answers the getters itself; callbacks can't
cross processes and are ignored. A swapchain keeps all its buffers, so
the buffer cache grows to 8 and the max buffer count it reports is
clamped to that.

The window surface clip that leaves the client area transparent is
applied when wineandroid.drv copies to the Android buffer, so win32u
now redraws the whole surface when the clip changes; otherwise what was
painted before the client surface appeared kept hiding it.
